GNU General Public License v2.0 or later, with the Bootloader Exception
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

PyInstaller itself is GPL-2.0-or-later. Two things are easy to get wrong here, so
they are stated separately:

  - What is embedded and GPL: the compiled bootloader (`bootloader/` and
    `PyInstaller/loader/`). It is linked into Setup.exe by design.
  - What is embedded and *not* GPL: PyInstaller's run-time hooks
    (`PyInstaller/hooks/rthooks`), `pyi_splash` and `_pyi_rth_utils` are Apache-2.0.
    The Community Hooks package likewise splits: its standard hooks are
    GPL-2.0-or-later but run only on the build host, while only its run-time hooks
    (`_pyinstaller_hooks_contrib/rthooks`, Apache-2.0) are embedded.

The exception, verbatim from PyInstaller's COPYING.txt:

    Bootloader Exception
    --------------------

    In addition to the permissions in the GNU General Public License, the
    authors give you unlimited permission to link or embed compiled bootloader
    and related files into combinations with other programs, and to distribute
    those combinations without any restriction coming from the use of those
    files. (The General Public License restrictions do apply in other respects;
    for example, they cover modification of the files, and distribution when
    not linked into a combined executable.)

Consequence for this product: Setup.exe is a combination that embeds the compiled
bootloader, so its distribution is permitted without restriction and no AIDriven
NetDefense source file becomes GPL-covered by the act of packaging. The GPL still
governs the bootloader files themselves: this project does not modify them, and it
does not distribute them outside a combined executable. The corresponding source
for the bootloader is the PyInstaller 6.22.3 source release (pinned in
`requirements.lock.txt`); this notice is also a written offer to supply it on
request through the project's public issue tracker.

GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or later
--------------------------------------------------------

py7zr and its four decoder dependencies (pybcj, pyppmd, inflate64,
multivolumefile) are LGPL-2.1-or-later and are frozen into Setup.exe. They are used
unmodified, at the versions pinned in `requirements.lock.txt`.

  - Source: each package's PyPI release ships the corresponding source, and the
    upstream repositories are https://github.com/miurahr/py7zr,
    https://github.com/miurahr/pybcj, https://github.com/miurahr/pyppmd,
    https://github.com/miurahr/inflate64 and
    https://github.com/miurahr/multivolume.
  - Copyright notices: held by the respective upstream authors; the licence texts
    are the ones shipped inside each wheel (`*.dist-info/licenses/LICENSE`).

Relinkability - the one obligation this packaging does not satisfy mechanically, and
the position taken instead. LGPL-2.1 section 6 expects a recipient to be able to
modify the library and relink to produce a modified executable. A `--onefile`
PyInstaller bundle is a single compressed archive; nothing inside it can be swapped
after the fact. What the project offers instead is the equivalent through a rebuild:
the complete installer source (`deploy/windows/installer/app/`), the exact pinned
resolution (`requirements.lock.txt`) and the build command are public, so anyone can
substitute a modified py7zr and rebuild Setup.exe with it. That is a documented
rebuild path, not a binary-level relink, and it is recorded here as such rather than
described as something stronger. Replacing the decoder is not blocked by any hash or
signature check at load time.

This position is an engineering judgement about how to meet the obligation, and it is
open for the maintainer to replace with a stronger remedy (for example shipping the
licence texts and the LGPL sources alongside the installer, or dropping py7zr for a
permissively licensed decoder).

This notice records an engineering compliance position, not a legal opinion.
